Your report must cover these topics:
1.	Your role and responsibilities this month (+ evidence)
 
 
 

During Month 1, I worked as a System Analyst at Safwa Islamic Bank's IT Department. My role focused on understanding business operations and building the data foundation needed to support the bank's digital transformation goals.

Weeks 1–2: Call Center Study & Systems Analysis
I studied the call center operations in detail to understand how customers interact with the bank. My work included:
	Analyzing the types of customer calls received and identifying the underlying reasons for each
	Identifying patterns and recurring themes across hundreds of customer interactions
	Grouping calls into clear service categories: Account Services, Complaints, General Inquiries, and Transaction-related requests
	Flagging repetitive and manual processes with potential for automation or digitization
	Documenting all findings in structured reports to support future system improvement decisions

Objective: Understand why customers contact the bank, identify high-frequency service types, and provide an evidence-based foundation for the deeper analysis in Month 2.

Weeks 3–4: Branch Data Analysis (44 Branches | Last 6 Years)
 
I expanded my scope to a large-scale data analysis exercise covering 44 branches over six years. Key activities included:
	Collecting and consolidating historical data from all 44 branches
	Cleaning and preparing raw data using Microsoft Excel
	Building interactive dashboards and visualizations in Microsoft Power BI
	Using Microsoft Copilot (AI assistance) to accelerate analysis and surface key trends
	Analyzing metrics: customers arrived vs. served, service time, waiting time, workload distribution, and service efficiency
	Comparing branch performance to identify overloaded branches, high-wait services, and efficiency gaps

2.	Any problems / Challenges and how did you overcome them?

	Unstructured call data – Customer calls were not categorized clearly in the source system. Overcome by applying pattern recognition and logical grouping to derive a clean classification framework.
	High variability in customer requests – Each call was unique, making pattern identification difficult. Resolved by focusing on frequency analysis and grouping similar intents together.
	Data inconsistency across 44 branches – Branch data had formatting inconsistencies. Resolved through a standardization process in Excel before loading into Power BI.
	Large dataset volume – Six years of data across 44 branches was resource-intensive. Managed by segmenting and processing data in structured batches.

